    "expensive_path": "Updating every collision geometry's placement from the kinematics and then running narrow-phase distance and collision queries over the collision-pair set, which grows as the square of the number of geometries before broadphase pruning. For a humanoid with self-collision pairs this dominates a planning query, and both the broadphase sweep and the independent narrow-phase queries parallelise naturally, which is why pinocchio already ships an OpenMP pool variant.",
    "rationale": "Collision is different physics from dynamics: distance fields and geometric predicates rather than equations of motion. It has its own model (GeometryModel and GeometryData), its own upstream test set, and its own consumers in motion planning.",
    "excluded": [
      "the coal library itself, which performs the narrow-phase query and is an external dependency, not part of the pinned tree",
      "mesh loading and the URDF geometry parser, which are shared infrastructure"
    ],
    "hazards": [
      "The expensive inner query lives in the external coal library, so a port can only accelerate pinocchio's placement update, pair management and broadphase unless coal is ported too. This bounds the achievable speed-up and the human should weigh it before approving the module.",
      "Collision results include discrete outcomes (in collision or not) whose flip near a tangency is not containable by a tolerance; witness points on a flat contact are non-unique.",
      "Every test in this module loads meshes from the example-robot-data submodule.",
      "Shares the unseeded-model hazard recorded on rigid-body-algorithms: buildModels::humanoidRandom randomises placements, inertias and limits from std::rand, so the model must be frozen as data in ic/."
